uh glenn how are you doing  good drew it's great to talk to you  great to talk to you last time i talked  to you you were had a  short film on a phone so i feel like  we've come a long way  since then um i want to ask first you  know  and without you know sending you back to  therapy but you  developed uh rapunzel for a long time as  a director  and i was wondering what you took from  that experience and applied  when finally getting to make over the  moon everything that i  loved about directing rapunzel and then  it became tangled  i just took it to the next level on this  movie  um really wanting the subtlety of  performance  in fifa's space and designing  characters that we're going to deliver  the thing that i i love in our  characters is that  believing the impossible is possible  animating  in her eyes that kind of  crazy drive of this little 12 year old  girl is gonna build a rocket to the moon  she's so smart she knows math and  science and physics  and yet also has this belief  from her mom this faith to see what  others don't see  that that's the joy that i wanted to  be able to to do in this movie was there  ever  talk of it being a 2d hand-drawn feature  well i was certainly given that choice  um  the wonderful thing about netflix and  is that they really give enormous  creative freedom  to you um and  i knew that there was one part that i  for sure wanted to do  by hand and that was this scarf  animation  when i read that i was like okay that  has got to be  that's mine i'm going to do that myself  for it i researched ancient chinese  drawings and really learned  the rhythms of the flow of of  those the scarf the movements in china  as she would fly through the air  and put that into the animation  but the thing that really drove me to go  cg  with this um was  what i saw in wizard of oz uh  wizard of oz in a lot of ways is a  a great um  example for our film where they went  from this black and white  to technicolor that  fantasy world was so remarkable and i  was thinking how in the world  are we going to do that and i remember  seeing  the um pink floyd's dark side of the  moon  album cover with that prism and the  white light  exploding into a  rainbow of colors and light it was about  light  and that's what this was going to be on  earth it was going to be  reflected light in this little town of  china  on the moon in the dark side of the moon  in lunaria it was going to be  source light exploding out from  the inside out and that  that made all the difference in the  world when celine de ramon a production  designer showed me the first image of  a in front of lunaria  i all i could do was laugh and cry  at the same time it was so beautiful you  could only do that in cg i'm assuming  that kind of illumination  right you can only do that in cg yeah  um can you talk about working with with  john because he's not here today but i  would always love to  i would love to hear what that dynamic  was like i mean you guys are  are uh illustrators who have used  computer  animation and la and hand-drawn sort of  together in really interesting ways  um what was that partnership like  halfway through the film  i realized how wonderful it would be to  have a partner like john  uh as this film was moving at such a  lightning speed  um to have someone who thought in many  ways  like i do and yet bring something  fresh and new his there was  there was one point in the movie palin  chow  melissa cobb um they would make  suggestions here and there and  i always had a choice of whether we'd do  it or not um  but at this point  i finally realized they were right i  needed to add this little  moment into the film where fei-fei is  running with this gift and  loses it that was  she hadn't lost it yet and we were we  were hearing from palin  she's got to lose it one one more time  and uh i realized that but we only had  like  five minutes left  to actually lock the film right  and so i quickly was storyboarding and  sketching as fast as i could what i  thought  and then then there's john sitting next  to me and he's drawing it  and then we both finished at the same  time we realized we had the same idea  but  his was like a bird's eye view looking  down  planning where all the cameras would go  mine was  the images of the character moments and  what the acting would be  and it was such a great compliment uh  john  is left-handed i'm right-handed as we  would be working with  the animators in vancouver  he and i would be sitting shoulder to  shoulder and some poor animator had both  of us  drawing together at the same time over  top of  their work um it was  it was a really wonderful partnership  with him  well can you talk a little bit about  sort of wrangling these different  animation studios and you've got pearl  in china and what that sort of was like  uh especially as a first-time feature  director i  imagine that was uh a lot  jenny rim is an incredible producer who  builds a team around you that's kind of  the perfect team for you it fits  like tailor-made clothes you feel like  you're surrounded by the best  and we tested various studios before we  went with sony  and worked with sasha coming to bangka  that was  and working with him was incredible he  has such  a um um  a sense of who the animators would be  the best ones  for us we really were searching for  as many people who had an asian  background as possible  to bring that sensitivity to it uh jenny  and palin were both  asian um all of our voice talent were  the artists in shanghai that really  helped me  a lot about acting choices  in this movie although it was being done  in vancouver um  we traveled back and forth many times  but on my wall in my office was a big  monitor and that was my window to the  world because we were working with  people in holland and  shanghai and france and spain  and and i just got to to go to all those  places  and it was great training for when the  pandemic hit that right  moving forward well glenn thank you so  much for taking the time to chat with me  i love the movie and it's always a  thrill to get to talk to you so  enjoy the rest of your day and hopefully  we'll talk soon yes thank you drew it's  great to see you again

